// THE VISUDO (SUDOERS-VALIDATE) OPERATION, UPSTREAM OF ITS CONSUMERS. Three sites validate sudoers
// files through the same two commands -- the deploy preamble's pre-install syntax check of a staged
// drop-in, its post-install policy check of the installed drop-in, and the microVM-controller
// install's staged check -- and each had been spelling the argv as shell text. This module is the
// single owner of the two typed commands so a future change to how the validate command renders
// reaches every consumer at once, and so the argv a consumer runs is the argv the witness below
// asserts, byte for byte.
//
// BOTH COMMANDS ARE ELEVATED: validating a file under /etc/sudoers.d requires root to read it, and
// the elevation is recorded in the argv itself -- /usr/bin/sudo, its -n (non-interactive) flag, and
// the visudo binary by absolute path. That is the same elevation shape extdeps.systemd.systemctl
// records for daemon-reload: the sudo prefix is the argument to argv_command, and the command the
// sudo runs is the tail. visudo is an ARGUMENT to sudo, not a program this repository execs, so it
// carries no ProgramIdentity row here and no entry in the program roster -- the only thing that
// execs is /usr/bin/sudo, already admitted.
//
// THE CHECK FLAG IS THE WHOLE POINT. visudo(8) -c is a check-only pass: it parses the file, prints
// any error with a line number, and exits non-zero on the first parse failure. It does not edit,
// does not require a tty, and does not lock. A consumer that needs the file to be syntactically
// valid runs this command and reads the exit code: zero is valid, anything else is a parse failure
// that must refuse the surrounding operation. The command never needs the output to decide, only
// the exit code, which is why the op is an ArgvCommand and not a shell-text builder: the typed
// transport reports the exit code, and a shell-text builder would force every consumer to re-parse
// a rendered command line to recover the words it just rendered.
//
// THE POLICY CHECK ENFORCES OWNER AND MODE, NOT ONLY SYNTAX. visudo(8): -O enforces the default
// owner and group of the file being checked, and -P its default mode, alongside -c. Without them a
// file sudo will IGNORE for its ownership (root:ghrunner, say) still passes -c, because the parse
// succeeds -- sudo refuses to read the file at run time, which is a different failure this check
// must surface in advance. The policy command is therefore -c -O -P -f <file>, every flag a
// separate word, and it is the check a consumer runs after installing a drop-in, not before,
// because only the installed location has the ownership the check is about.
//
// THE BINARY PATH IS /usr/sbin/visudo, the same value gunbc.fleet.fleet_posix_accounts records in
// its NOPASSWD grant roster as fleet_visudo_binary_path. extdeps cannot import gunbc.fleet, so the
// row is spelled here and the witness below asserts the two agree in value, which is the strongest
// coupling the dependency direction allows: if the fleet roster moves the binary, the witness
// breaks, and the two spellings cannot silently disagree.

module extdeps.sudo.visudo

import std.types { NonEmptyStr, String }
import extdeps.version { VersionConstraint }
import extdeps.external_authority { ExternalAuthority }
import extdeps.uri { Uri, Https }
import extdeps.exec.command { ArgvCommand, argv_command }
import extdeps.sudo.elevation { sudo_program, sudo_non_interactive_flag }

// visudo(8), the sudoers-file editor and validator. The man page is the authority for the flag
// vocabulary this module records; the live page (1.8 series, through 1.8.27) documents -c, -f, -O
// and -P, which is every flag the two commands below use. The anchor is the same URL shape the
// rest of extdeps uses for a single man page: the page itself, not a section of a book.
data extdeps_external_authority_anchor: ExternalAuthority = ExternalAuthority {
  uri: Uri {
    scheme: Https
    locator: "www.sudo.ws/docs/man/visudo.man/"
  }
}

data visudo_authority: ExternalAuthority = extdeps_external_authority_anchor

// THE MINIMUM VERSION IS A DOCUMENTARY FLOOR, NOT A RUNTIME PROBE. No consumer in this repository
// runs `visudo --version` and branches on the result; the row exists so the flag vocabulary this
// module records is tied to a version of the tool the cited page documents, the way
// min_coreutils_version ties the coreutils flags to a coreutils release. The floor is 1.8.4, the
// release the live visudo(8) page names as the one that added the check pass this module's -c
// spelling relies on ("visudo versions 1.8.4 and higher" support the flexible check/debug pass the
// page documents); the page's SYNOPSIS is visudo [-chIOPqsV] [[-f] sudoers], so every flag the two
// commands below use is in that option set. A host running a sudo older than the 1.8 series could
// in principle lack the check-pass behavior; none of the three consumers run on such a host, and
// the refusal that surfaces on one is the non-zero exit from visudo itself, not a missing-flag
// error this module would need to model.
data min_visudo_version: VersionConstraint = ">= 1.8.4"

// THE BINARY PATH, /usr/sbin/visudo, where the sudo package ships visudo on every distribution
// this repository deploys to. It is the value gunbc.fleet.fleet_posix_accounts records as
// fleet_visudo_binary_path in its NOPASSWD grant roster; extdeps cannot import gunbc.fleet, so the
// value is spelled here and the witness below asserts the two agree, the strongest coupling the
// dependency direction allows. Spelled as one literal rather than a directory-and-name join: the
// path is the fact, not two facts joined, and the fleet roster spells it the same way.
data visudo_binary_path: NonEmptyStr = "/usr/sbin/visudo"
data visudo_binary_name: NonEmptyStr = "visudo"

// THE SYNTAX CHECK: `sudo -n /usr/sbin/visudo -c -f <file>`. -c is check-only, -f names the file
// to check instead of the default /etc/sudoers. The flag is the single word `-cf`, the combined
// form visudo(8) documents, not two words: the deploy preamble's shell-text builder spelled it
// `-cf` and this op must render the same argv the shell rendered, byte for byte, so a consumer
// that swaps the shell builder for this op runs the identical command.
fn visudo_check_command(file: String) -> ArgvCommand {
  argv_command(
    program: sudo_program(),
    arguments: [
      sudo_non_interactive_flag as String,
      visudo_binary_path as String,
      "-cf",
      file,
    ],
  )
}

// THE POLICY CHECK: `sudo -n /usr/sbin/visudo -c -O -P -f <file>`. -c parses, -O enforces the
// default owner and group, -P enforces the default mode, -f names the file. Every flag is a
// separate word here, the form the deploy preamble's installed-location builder spelled, and the
// reason -O and -P are separate rather than folded is that they are independent enforcement rules
// a consumer might one day want to run one without the other; the combined form is not in
// visudo(8) for these two. The check runs against the installed file, where ownership is the fact
// under test, not the staged file, where it is not set yet.
fn visudo_policy_check_command(file: String) -> ArgvCommand {
  argv_command(
    program: sudo_program(),
    arguments: [
      sudo_non_interactive_flag as String,
      visudo_binary_path as String,
      "-c",
      "-O",
      "-P",
      "-f",
      file,
    ],
  )
}
